@flexiarg math-formalization/search-the-namespace-not-the-qualified-name
@title Search the Namespace, Not the Qualified Name
@sigils [🔍/名]
@keywords search, grep, namespace, declaration, Mathlib, discovery, def, lemma, qualified, bare
@audience lean-provers, agents
@tone heuristic
@difficulty easy
@subjects formalization tooling
@examples b01A04
@provenance case-1 compression pilot (2026-08-12): three-way convergence — operator's self-diagnosed error, supervisor memory (grep-anchoring-hides-namespaced-decls), independent zai-1 mark

! conclusion:
  Mathlib declares most names INSIDE namespace blocks, so grep for the
  BARE name within candidate files (or use exact-name search tools);
  a qualified-name anchor like `^def IntermediateField.restrict` returns
  empty on a declaration that exists, and an empty result read as
  absence sends the proof down an unnecessary construction.

  + context: You believe an API should exist (restrict, lift, comap
    variants), grep for its qualified name, find nothing, and conclude
    you must build it — or recommend the wrong neighboring API.

  + IF: You are searching Lean/Mathlib source with text tools; the
    target is plausibly declared as `def restrict` inside
    `namespace IntermediateField ... end`.

  + HOWEVER: Bare-name search over-returns (every namespace's `restrict`);
    filter by file/namespace context. And an empty result is still only
    evidence about your QUERY, not about the library — corroborate with
    a second modality (exact-name search, doc-gen, or reading the
    likeliest file's declaration list) before acting on absence.

  + THEN: grep -n "^def restrict\|^lemma restrict" within the namespace's
    file; or search doc-gen/loogle by exact name; before recommending an
    API to another agent, verify it by opening the file at the line.

  + BECAUSE: Empty-as-absence is the retrieval analogue of the silence
    failure mode: a well-formed query with a wrong anchor produces a
    confident wrong world-model, and the cost lands downstream in
    someone else's round.
